{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe outbound Jira comment dedup key is one composition of functions in the differ and the send is a different composition in the adapter, so every convergence bug has been a point where the two drifted: `fit_comment_as_sent` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_comments.py:283`) re-runs the send's fit to stay equal to `acli_cli_ops.add_comment:531`; the key-side `codec.normalize_outbound(...)` at `outbound_comments.py:572` is still an unmatched extra step; the truncation suffix literal lives in three places (`adapters/jira/comment_limits.py:46`, `adf.py:333`, `jira_family/rich_text.py:69`) and the limit in four (32767 ×3 vs 32000 at `adf.py:332`); decoration happens in the differ and fitting in the adapter, which sheared the marker (`unreal-empathetic-illadopsis`). Landed fixes `8209097fa9` (one decoder) and `46575522d6` (DC fit on the send path) corrected two drifts; the structure that produced five tickets (`irongrey-chubby-oxpecker`, `abusive-pebbly-sakimonkey`, `aragonite-bleached-vipersquid`, `unreal-empathetic-illadopsis`, `community-sediment-snipe`) remains. Open story `marshy-chummy-coot` is the escape analysis of the first; this story is the structural remedy.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `fit_for_send(body, codec, fit) -> FittedComment(landed_text, wire_body)` in `outbound_comments.py` (core names no vendor; `fit` injected): `wire_body = fit_preserving_marker(decorate(body), fit)` computed once, `landed_text = strip_marker(codec.decode_inbound(codec.to_wire(wire_body)))`. The differ keys on `landed_text`; the mutation carries `wire_body`; Cloud and DC `add_comment` only encode and send. One suffix and one limit constant in `jira_family/rich_text.py`; delete the copies. Guard: the suffix literal and the 32767/32000 constants appear once in `src/rebar`.\n\n\n## Guard construction\nFollow the guard-test construction rule in the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner module exempt, match the constituent conjunction or AST shape named in Approach (or the definition count where no distinctive atom exists), `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape marker, and behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_comments.py`, `adapters/jira/backend.py`, `adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py`, `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py`, `adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py`, `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py`, `adapters/jira/adf.py`, `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_fit_for_send.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nHappy path over the captured corpus `tests/fixtures/cloud_adf_corpus/bodies_*.json` (from `scripts/capture_jira_fixtures.py`): for every body, `landed_text` equals the decoded readback of `wire_body`. Edge: a body over the limit keeps the marker and the key still matches; DC bodies fit at `comment_max_chars()`. Pinning: `tests/unit/test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py`, `test_dc_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py`, `test_outbound_comment_marker_survives_truncation.py`, `test_acli_comment_adf_encode.py`, `test_rich_text_codec.py`, `test_backend_neutrality.py` pass unchanged.\n\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-21) — four blocking gaps closed\n\n### 1. The `32767` oracle was wrong, and the constant is not the construct\n`32767` occurs in FOUR files under `src/rebar/_engine`, and most occurrences are legitimate\nand must survive:\n- `adapters/jira/comment_limits.py:41` `_JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS` — Cloud's limit (in scope);\n- `rebar_reconciler/runtime.py:46` `_DEFAULT_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS` — the runtime default;\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/settings.py:60,64` — the DC `jira.text.field.character.limit`\n  `default-value`, quoted from Atlassian's own documentation;\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:244-257` — PROSE citing JRASERVER-28519 for why 32767 is\n  the DC default and why it is only a default.\n\nDeleting those would delete sourced product knowledge. The construct being consolidated is the\n**fit-for-send composition**, not the number. AC-2 is therefore rewritten to assert that the\n**dedup key is derived from the bytes actually sent** — an observable behavioural property —\nrather than to count occurrences of a magic number.\n\n### 2. The mutation-enactment consumer is now in Scope\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py:368-379` reads `entry.get(\"body\", \"\")` and calls\n`client.add_comment(jira_key, body)`. Moving fit/decorate/encode out of `add_comment` and into\nmutation construction changes what this site must pass, so it is added to Scope: the mutation\nentry gains `wire_body`, and this call site passes `entry[\"wire_body\"]` (falling back to\n`body` only for entries written before the cutover, so an in-flight queue does not break).\n\n### 3. The `fit_comment` port and BOTH implementers are in Scope\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py:481` declares the `fit_comment(self, body: str) -> str`\nProtocol whose contract is \"return exactly the marker-free body the backend's SEND path lands\".\nImplementers: `adapters/jira/backend.py:154` (Cloud) and\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:303` (DC), both delegating to\n`outbound_comments.fit_comment_as_sent` (`outbound_comments.py:290`).\n\n**Decision: `fit_comment_as_sent` is RETAINED as a thin wrapper** — `fit_for_send(...).landed_text`\n— so the Protocol's `-> str` signature and both implementers keep working unchanged. Only\ncallers that need the wire bytes move to `fit_for_send`. This keeps the port stable and confines\nthe change to the dedup-key path.\n\n### 4. One codec, one set of bytes — the drift this leaf exists to close\nThe plan said adapters \"only encode and send\" without saying how. Specifically:\n- `acli_cli_ops.add_comment:531`, `adapters/jira/backend.add_comment` and DC's\n  `sanitize_comment` **stop fitting/encoding independently** — each currently re-derives the\n  body, which is exactly how the key and the sent bytes drift apart.\n- `wire_body` is carried on the mutation entry (see §2), so the bytes are computed once.\n- The send path MUST NOT re-encode: it writes `wire_body` verbatim. Where an adapter genuinely\n  must encode at send time, it receives **the same codec instance** that\n  `fit_for_send` was given, and the leaf asserts that identity in a test — a second codec\n  instance, or a second read of the cutover flag, re-opens the drift.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] For every corpus body, key equals readback; checked by the new corpus test.\n- [ ] `grep -rn \"32767\\|32000\" src/rebar/_engine` returns one file; checked by the grep and the guard test.\n- [ ] Pinning suites pass unchanged; checked by `pytest`.\n- [ ] The dedup key equals a hash of the bytes actually handed to the transport; checked by a test that fits an over-long comment and asserts key == hash(wire_body) == hash(what the fake transport received).\n- [ ] `add_comment` performs no independent fit/encode; checked by a test asserting a fake codec is invoked exactly once per outbound comment.\n- [ ] The send path uses the SAME codec instance (or the precomputed `wire_body`) as `fit_for_send`; checked by an identity assertion in that test.\n- [ ] `fit_comment_as_sent` and the `_backend.fit_comment` Protocol keep their `-> str` contract; checked by both backend implementers passing unchanged.\n- [ ] A mutation entry written before the cutover (no `wire_body`) still sends; checked by a back-compat test at `dispatch_one`.\n- [ ] The sourced `32767` occurrences in `settings.py`, `backend.py` prose and `runtime.py` are unchanged; checked by `git diff --stat`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787365740201575001,"uuid":"50cf45a5-8d0e-4bb1-a90f-5dab0e40478d"}